# ============================================================================
# Kazhal API — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY — never commit a real secret.
# Every variable below is validated at boot by src/configs/env.validation.ts;
# a missing or malformed REQUIRED variable aborts startup with its name.
#
# Generate the two secrets with:   openssl rand -hex 32
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- runtime
# development | test | production
# production additionally disables Swagger (/v1/api) and OTP debug logging.
NODE_ENV=development

# Optional. Default: 8000 (the local-development contract: API 8000, Web 8001, POS 8002).
PORT=8000

# Optional. Comma-separated allowed browser origins for HTTP CORS *and* the
# websocket gateway. Empty (the default when unset) means no cross-origin
# access at all. The value below is for local development: Web and admin on
# 8001, POS on 8002. In production list the real origins instead, e.g.
# https://admin.kazhal.ir,https://kazhal.ir
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8001,http://localhost:8002

# --------------------------------------------------------------- security
# REQUIRED. Signs both access and refresh JWTs. Min 16 chars; use 32+ bytes.
JWT_SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# REQUIRED. HMAC key for OTP hashes stored in Redis. Min 16 chars.
OTP_HMAC_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Optional. `ms` timespan. Default: 15m
# Also used verbatim as the access_token cookie maxAge — keep them identical.
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRATION=15m

# Optional. `ms` timespan. Default: 2d
# Also used as the refresh_token cookie maxAge.
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRATION=2d

# --------------------------------------------------------------- database
# All REQUIRED. DB_PASSWORD may be empty, but must be present:
#   DB_PASSWORD=
# The values below are the canonical local stack (`pnpm infra:up`, Compose
# project `kazhal-review`). They are throwaway local-only credentials from
# docker-compose.review.yml, never production ones.
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=55433
DB_USERNAME=kazhal_review
DB_PASSWORD=kazhal_review_local_only
DB_DATABASE=kazhal_review

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ redis
# Used for OTP storage and caching. Canonical local stack: 127.0.0.1:56379.
REDIS_HOST=127.0.0.1
REDIS_PORT=56379

# --------------------------------------------------------------- business
# Optional. IANA timezone for report calendar-day boundaries.
# Default: Asia/Tehran
REPORT_TIMEZONE=Asia/Tehran

# Optional. How long stock stays allocated to a created-but-unpaid order.
# Default: 1800000 (30 minutes) — provisional, see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md D-2.
ORDER_ALLOCATION_TTL_MS=1800000

# Optional. How long a checkout quote stays acceptable, in milliseconds.
# Default: 900000 (15 minutes).
#
# A quote is the customer's accepted purchase — the exact SKUs, quantities,
# unit prices and total they agreed to buy — signed by the server and required
# by POST /orders. It is a REVIEW WINDOW, not a stock hold: stock is held by
# the four-hour basket reservations and a quote confers no priority over an
# in-store sale, so a stale quote is refused with a fresh one rather than
# honoured. Clamped at boot to [30000, BASKET_RESERVATION_TTL_MS]: a quote
# must never outlive the reservation it prices.
CHECKOUT_QUOTE_TTL_MS=900000

# ----------------------------------------------------- object storage (images)
# Product image upload. ALL FIVE of the first block are REQUIRED for uploads to
# work; without them the API still starts in development and answers 503
# IMAGE_STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE on POST /products/uploadImage, while under
# NODE_ENV=production it REFUSES TO START and names what is missing.
#
# The local stack that satisfies these is the canonical one, from the
# repository root:
#   pnpm infra:up      # PostgreSQL, Redis and MinIO (+ bucket provisioning)
#   pnpm infra:ps      # health
#   pnpm infra:stop    # stop, keep data
# MinIO API on http://localhost:59000, console on http://localhost:59001, with
# the credentials below. `pnpm infra:*` always selects the `kazhal-review`
# project; manual `docker compose` commands bypass it (the base file on its own
# creates a separate, empty `api` project on port 9000), so do not use them for
# normal local development.

# The endpoint the API signs and uploads against. INTERNAL — in a real
# deployment this is a private address (a service-mesh name, a VPC endpoint)
# that no browser can reach and none should be told about.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:59000

# The base URL a BROWSER fetches images from. The object key is appended to it
# with a single "/", and nothing else is added.
#
#   path-style MinIO : http://localhost:59000/kazhal-products  (bucket included)
#   a CDN at the root: https://cdn.example.com                 (bucket omitted)
#
# THIS IS NOT THE SAME AS S3_ENDPOINT and must not be set to it out of habit.
# They coincide only on a laptop; treating them as one value is what made
# stored URLs unreachable in every other environment.
#
# ⚠ AN OWNER DECISION IS OUTSTANDING FOR PRODUCTION. The local bucket is
# configured for ANONYMOUS DOWNLOAD by `minio-init` (`mc anonymous set
# download`) so a browser can display images with no credentials. That is a
# DEVELOPMENT convenience and nothing here assumes it in production, where the
# choice is between:
#
#   (a) a PUBLIC bucket or CDN — this variable points at the CDN origin, image
#       URLs are plain and cacheable, and anyone with a URL can fetch the
#       object. Simplest, and normal for product photography.
#   (b) a PRIVATE bucket with SIGNED or PROXIED delivery — URLs expire or are
#       served through the API. Needed only if product images must not be
#       publicly reachable. This would require a signing/proxy step that is NOT
#       implemented; the stored `url` column assumes a directly fetchable
#       address.
#
# Until that is decided, do not point this at a production bucket.
S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:59000/kazhal-products

# PLACEHOLDERS — the canonical local stack's MinIO root user from
# docker-compose.review.yml. Never a real key.
S3_ACCESS_KEY=kazhal_review
S3_SECRET_KEY=kazhal_review_local_only

# Must already exist. The API never creates a bucket: a typo in this name would
# otherwise become a brand-new empty bucket that silently accepts uploads
# nobody can find. The `minio-init` compose service provisions it locally.
S3_BUCKET=kazhal-products

# Optional. Default: us-east-1. Meaningless to MinIO, required by the signer.
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# Optional. Default: true. Path-style addressing (host/bucket/key), which MinIO
# and most S3-compatible servers need. Set false for AWS S3 itself.
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Optional. Default: 5242880 (5 MiB). Refused with 413 above this. Must be
# between 1024 and 67108864 — the upper bound exists because the whole file is
# buffered in memory during the request.
PRODUCT_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES=5242880

# --- legacy spellings, still read ---
# MINIO_ENDPOINT, MINIO_ACCESS_KEY, MINIO_SECRET_KEY and MINIO_BUCKET are
# accepted as fallbacks for S3_ENDPOINT / S3_ACCESS_KEY / S3_SECRET_KEY /
# S3_BUCKET so an existing deployment does not break. S3_* WINS wherever both
# are set, and every legacy name in use is reported once at boot. There is no
# legacy spelling for S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL, because no old variable meant it.

# --------------------------------------------- object deletion (durable)
# When an image is removed from a product, or an abandoned upload is swept, a
# row is written to `object_deletion_jobs` IN THE SAME TRANSACTION that removes
# the image row. That is what makes a failed S3 delete a retry instead of a
# permanent leak: before this existed, the key lived only in a local variable
# once the row was gone, and a failed delete left bytes nothing could name.
#
# This worker is ON by default, unlike the orphan sweeper below. The two decide
# different things: the sweeper decides that an image is ABANDONED, which needs
# a policy nobody has set; this worker only finishes deletions somebody already
# decided on. Turning it off is not a conservative default, it is a guaranteed
# leak — so it is only here as an escape hatch.
#
# Retries use capped exponential backoff and stop after 10 attempts, at which
# point the job is marked `abandoned` and KEPT, because it is the only record
# that those bytes are unreferenced and still present.
OBJECT_DELETION_ENABLED=true
# Optional. Default: 60000 (1 minute).
OBJECT_DELETION_INTERVAL_MS=60000
# Optional. Default: 25 jobs per pass.
OBJECT_DELETION_BATCH_SIZE=25

# ------------------------------------------ orphaned image cleanup (OFF)
# A detached image — uploaded, never attached to a product — is left forever
# unless this is enabled. The sweeper is implemented and tested; it is OFF
# because the TTL is a BUSINESS DECISION THE OWNER HAS NOT MADE.
#
# Too short deletes photos out of a form somebody left open over lunch; too
# long grows the bucket. Setting PRODUCT_IMAGE_CLEANUP_ENABLED=true WITHOUT
# PRODUCT_IMAGE_ORPHAN_TTL_MS is refused at boot rather than defaulted.
#
# An attached image is never deleted by this, under any setting.
PRODUCT_IMAGE_CLEANUP_ENABLED=false
# RECOMMENDED: 86400000 (24 hours) for abandoned unattached uploads. That is a
# RECOMMENDATION ONLY and is deliberately left commented out — cleanup stays
# disabled until the owner approves a value, because deleting a photo out of a
# form somebody left open is not recoverable by rerunning anything.
# PRODUCT_IMAGE_ORPHAN_TTL_MS=86400000
# PRODUCT_IMAGE_CLEANUP_INTERVAL_MS=3600000
# PRODUCT_IMAGE_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE=100

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ payment
# Optional. `stub` (default in test) or `zibal`. The stub NEVER contacts a real
# gateway and is what the whole integration suite runs against. The zibal
# driver has NOT been exercised against the live service — see
# docs/VERIFICATION.md §4 before enabling it.
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_DRIVER=stub
# The gateway redirects the customer's BROWSER here (a GET), so it must be the
# Web storefront's return page, which then calls POST /payment/verify itself.
# Never point it at the API's /payment/verify: that route is POST-only.
PAYMENT_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:8001/checkout/payment/return
# Optional. Milliseconds.
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# How long an unfinished attempt may be reused before it is written off.
PAYMENT_PENDING_REUSE_MS=900000
PAYMENT_MAX_PENDING_PER_ORDER=5
# Concurrent `Start()`: how long a second caller waits for the first caller's
# in-flight gateway session instead of opening a second one.
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_INFLIGHT_WINDOW_MS=30000
PAYMENT_START_INFLIGHT_WAIT_MS=5000
PAYMENT_START_INFLIGHT_POLL_MS=100

# Re-verification sweep: asks the gateway about attempts that have a track_id
# but never reached a terminal state (lost callback, or a crash after the
# gateway confirmed but before we recorded it). See docs/VERIFICATION.md for
# exactly what this does and does NOT recover.
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_ENABLED=true
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_INTERVAL_MS=60000
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_BATCH_SIZE=20
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_AFTER_MS=120000
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_MAX_AGE_MS=604800000
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=8
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_BACKOFF_BASE_MS=60000
PAYMENT_REVERIFY_BACKOFF_MAX_MS=3600000

# ------------------------------------------------------------- fulfilment
# Phase-2 recovery worker. Fair, due-time scheduling with capped backoff, so a
# permanently unfulfillable order cannot starve later ones.
FULFILMENT_RECOVERY_ENABLED=true
FULFILMENT_RECOVERY_INTERVAL_MS=30000
FULFILMENT_RECOVERY_BATCH_SIZE=10
FULFILMENT_RECOVERY_BACKOFF_BASE_MS=60000
FULFILMENT_RECOVERY_BACKOFF_MAX_MS=3600000
# Attempts after which an order is reported in RecoveryReport.overdue and
# logged for a human. It is still retried — abandoning a PAID order is a
# financial decision the owner has not made (D-2).
FULFILMENT_RECOVERY_ALERT_ATTEMPTS=5

# ------------------------------------------- PROVISIONAL / UNAPPROVED POLICY
# ⚠ NOT APPROVED BY THE OWNER. Leave OFF.
#
# When true, a gateway confirmation that arrives AFTER the attempt was written
# off settles the order anyway. Off (the default) the receipt is recorded as
# `confirmed_unapplied` and flagged for human reconciliation, and no money
# moves. Under NODE_ENV=production this ALSO requires
# PROVISIONAL_PAYMENT_POLICY_ACK, or activation is refused and logged as an
# error on every evaluation. See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md D-2.
PAYMENT_LATE_CONFIRMATION_APPLIES=false
# PROVISIONAL_PAYMENT_POLICY_ACK=i-accept-unapproved-policy

# -------------------------------------------------------------- bootstrap
# Optional. When BOTH are set, the seeder creates a single super_admin on first
# run and never touches it again. Leave unset to skip admin bootstrapping.
# Change the password through the API immediately after first login.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=replace-me-min-8-chars

# Optional. Set to `false` to skip seeding on application bootstrap.
SEED_ON_BOOTSTRAP=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------- OTP SMS delivery
# Optional. `log` (default) or `kavenegar`.
#   log       — no SMS is ever sent. Outside production the code is discarded
#               and a masked "issued" line is logged. UNDER NODE_ENV=production
#               EVERY send-otp ANSWERS 503 (OTP_DELIVERY_FAILED) — it never
#               reports success — so it only serves as an emergency rollback
#               that switches SMS login off; a boot warning says so.
#   kavenegar — codes are delivered through Kavenegar's Verify/Lookup API.
#               KAVENEGAR_LOOKUP_URL becomes REQUIRED and is validated at boot;
#               an invalid value refuses to start and names the variable (never
#               its value).
OTP_SMS_DRIVER=log

# SECRET — the full lookup endpoint, which CONTAINS THE API KEY in its path:
#   https://api.kavenegar.com/v1/<API_KEY>/verify/lookup.json
# https only (plain http is accepted solely for a localhost stub outside
# production). No query string. Never commit a real value.
# KAVENEGAR_LOOKUP_URL=https://api.kavenegar.com/v1/<API_KEY>/verify/lookup.json

# Optional. Default: loginVerify — TEMPORARILY reusing the existing, approved
# template on the shared Kavenegar account for customer testing (owner
# decision). Only %token% is filled (the 6-digit code). Set this to a
# Kazhal-specific template name later; no code change is needed.
# Letters, digits, _ and -.
# KAVENEGAR_OTP_TEMPLATE=loginVerify

# --------------------------------------------------- storefront review door
# Optional, and OFF unless set to exactly `true`.
#
# Production customer authentication is a one-time code sent by SMS, and stays
# that way. `POST /auth/storefront/review-login` is a PASSWORD door that exists
# only so the shop can be signed into while no SMS provider is configured — for
# a review, a demo, or an automated test. It answers 403 unless BOTH conditions
# hold: NODE_ENV is not `production`, AND this variable is `true`. Either one
# alone is an accident waiting to ship (see `review-login.policy.ts`).
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET IN ANY ENVIRONMENT REAL CUSTOMERS CAN REACH. It was previously
# absent from this file entirely, which meant the documented review workflow in
# docs/AUTH-AUDIENCES.md had no corresponding setting anybody could find — and
# the integration suite, which signs customers in, sets it itself for the same
# reason.
# KAZHAL_ENABLE_REVIEW_LOGIN=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ misc
# Optional. Set to `true` to log every SQL statement from the TypeORM CLI.
TYPEORM_LOGGING=false
